course: geography
lesson: 41
title: Inside the Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Earth is not solid rock all the way through: it is built in layers, a bit like a peach with a thin skin, thick flesh and a hard stone in the middle.
explain: The skin is the crust we live on, the flesh is the mantle of hot rock, and the stone is the core of iron and nickel, liquid outside and solid inside.
explain: Think of a Scotch egg cut in half at a picnic: the breadcrumb crust is thin, the sausage meat is thick, and the yolk sits in the centre; the real crust is far thinner than the breadcrumbs compared with the whole.
explain: Nobody has ever dug down to the mantle, so scientists "listen" to earthquake waves passing through the planet, which bend, speed up, slow down or stop as they cross the different layers.
explain: The inside is hot because of heat left from when the Earth formed and because radioactive elements in the rock slowly break down and give off heat.
fact: The Earth is built in layers: a thin rocky crust, a thick hot mantle, a liquid outer core and a solid inner core.
fact: The crust is only about 5-10 km thick under the oceans and about 30-70 km thick under the continents.
fact: The mantle is about 2,900 km thick and is solid rock, but over millions of years it slowly flows like very stiff putty; only small pockets of it melt into runny rock.
fact: The outer core is liquid iron and nickel, and its swirling makes the Earth's magnetic field, the one a compass needle feels.
fact: The inner core is a solid ball of iron and nickel about 2,400 km across (a radius of about 1,200 km), roughly as hot as the surface of the Sun, about 5,000-6,000 °C, even hotter than the liquid outer core around it.
fact: The inner core stays solid despite the heat because the enormous pressure of everything above squeezes it.
fact: Nobody has ever been to the mantle or the core; we know about them by studying how earthquake waves travel through the Earth.
fact: Some earthquake waves (S waves) cannot pass through liquid, and the way they vanish on the far side of the Earth showed that the outer core is liquid.
fact: The deepest hole ever drilled, the Kola Superdeep Borehole in Russia, reached about 12 km, not even halfway through the continental crust there.
fact: The heat inside the Earth comes partly from when the planet formed and partly from radioactive elements slowly breaking down.
fact: The crust and the stiff top of the mantle together form the lithosphere, the hard shell that is broken into plates.
fact: The distance to the centre of the Earth is about 6,370 km, a bit more than the distance from London to New York (about 5,600 km).
exercise: What are the Earth's layers, from the outside in? | Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core | Mantle, crust, inner core, outer core | Crust, outer core, mantle, inner core | Crust, mantle, inner core, outer core
exercise: Where is the crust thinnest? | Under the oceans, about 5-10 km | Under the continents, about 30-70 km | Under high mountains, about 1 km | At the poles, where it is only ice
exercise: How thick is the crust under the continents? | About 30-70 km | About 2,900 km thick | About 1 metre or less | About 6,370 km in all
exercise: What is the mantle mostly like? | Solid hot rock that flows very slowly, like stiff putty | A great sea of runny red lava that sloshes about like hot soup | A hollow space full of gas | A layer of liquid iron and nickel
exercise: Roughly how thick is the mantle? | About 2,900 km | About 12 km thick | About 50 km in all | About 100,000 km
exercise: What is the outer core made of? | Liquid iron and nickel | Solid granite and basalt | Hot water and steam | Liquid gold and silver
exercise: What does the swirling of the liquid outer core make? | The Earth's magnetic field | The Earth's day and night | The tides of the sea | The Earth's seasons and its weather
exercise: How hot is the inner core, roughly? | About 5,000-6,000 °C, like the Sun's surface | About 100 °C, like boiling water in a kettle | About 500 °C, like a pizza oven | About a million °C, hotter than any star in the sky
exercise: Why is the inner core solid even though it is so hot? | The huge pressure from above squeezes it solid | It is made of ice, deep inside the hot rock | It is much cooler than the outer core around it | It is made of a kind of stone that can never melt at all
exercise: How do scientists know what is inside the Earth? | By studying how earthquake waves travel through it | By sending explorers down to the mantle | By drilling holes right through the mantle to the core | By looking at photos taken from space
exercise: How deep is the deepest hole ever drilled? | About 12 km, still inside the crust | About 600 km, deep in the mantle | About 2,900 km, right down to the core | About 6,370 km, to the very centre
exercise: Where does the heat inside the Earth come from? | Heat left from the Earth's birth and radioactive elements breaking down | Sunlight soaking down through the rocks over millions of years, a little each day | Fires burning coal deep underground | Friction from the Moon pulling the oceans
exercise: What is the lithosphere? | The crust plus the stiff top of the mantle, broken into plates | The liquid layer of iron and nickel that flows around the solid core | The air that surrounds the Earth | The layer of water covering the oceans
exercise: How far is it to the centre of the Earth? | About 6,370 km | About 637 km down | About 63,700 km | About 12 km, no more
exercise: How was it found that the outer core is liquid? | S waves cannot cross liquid and vanish on the far side | A deep drill brought back a sample of liquid metal from it | Volcanoes pour out core metal as lava | Astronauts saw it glowing from space
exercise: Which layer is by far the thinnest? | The crust | The mantle | The outer core | The inner core
test: Jack says: "Under the ground it's all lava, like a giant soup, that's why volcanoes happen." Put him right. | The mantle is solid rock that flows very slowly; only small pockets melt | He's right, the whole inside of the Earth is runny lava, all the way down to the centre | Under the ground it's all solid iron | Under the ground is a big empty cave
test: In a cartoon, two kids dig a hole in their garden all the way through to Australia. What's wrong with it? | The crust alone is tens of km thick and the core is thousands of degrees | Nothing, you would just need a long spade and a few weeks of hard digging | You'd come out in China, not Australia | The hole would fill with seawater at once
test: "if u could drive a car straight down at 100 km/h, how long to the centre?" | About 64 hours, almost three days | About 6 hours, less than a day | About 10 minutes | About a year
test: What would happen to compasses if the outer core stopped swirling? | The magnetic field would fade and compasses would stop working | Compasses would all point to the Sun instead of to the north | Compasses would spin faster and faster | Nothing, compasses work by gravity alone, not by magnetism
test: Mia reckons the core must be the coldest place because it's furthest from the Sun. Right? | No, it's about 5,000-6,000 °C from the Earth's own heat | Yes, it's frozen solid iron | Yes, it's about the same temperature as the North Pole in winter | No, it's only as warm as a summer day
test: "bloody hell, they drilled 12 km and didn't even get through the crust??" Is that true? | Yes, at Kola the continental crust is much thicker than 12 km | No, they went right through the mantle and touched the outer core | No, they reached the mantle after 2 km | No, the hole was only 12 metres deep
test: In a video game, the core of the planet is a giant ball of ice. What is the real core like? | Very hot iron and nickel, liquid outside and solid inside | Very hot rock that is liquid all through | A ball of gas lighter than air | A frozen lake of water and ice
test: Which is true about the inner core? | It is solid even though it is hotter than the liquid outer core | It is liquid because it is the hottest and deepest part of the Earth | It is hollow and full of air | It is made of the same rock as the crust
test: A scientist sees earthquake waves bend and change speed deep down. What does that tell her? | The waves are crossing into a layer of different material | The Earth is hollow there | The earthquake has stopped | The waves are bouncing off the Moon
test: Your little brother asks: "Are the plates under the oceans made of just the crust?" What's the best answer? | No, plates are the crust plus the stiff top of the mantle | Yes, plates are only the thin crust under the sea floor | No, plates are pieces of the outer core | No, plates are made of seawater ice
test: Why is the Earth still hot inside after billions of years? | Radioactive elements keep making heat, and a planet that big cools very slowly | The Sun heats it from the inside every day | Volcanoes pump heat back down into it | Burning oil underground keeps it warm
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the internal structure of the Earth? | A thin crust overlies a thick mantle, beneath which lie a liquid outer core and a solid inner core | A thick crust overlies a liquid mantle, beneath which lie a solid outer core and a hollow inner region | The planet consists of homogeneous solid rock from surface to centre | A molten mantle encloses an inner core composed principally of ice
exam: Identify the principal means by which the composition of the Earth's interior has been ascertained. | Analysis of the propagation of seismic waves through the planet | Direct sampling of the core by means of deep boreholes drilled for the purpose | Observation of the interior through volcanic craters | Photographic examination of the planet from orbit
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the Earth's magnetic field. | It is generated by the motion of molten iron and nickel within the outer core | It arises from magnetised rock in the oceanic crust alone | It is induced in the planet by the light of the Sun | It results from the gravitational attraction of the Moon
exam: It is given that the Kola Superdeep Borehole attained a depth of approximately twelve kilometres. Ascertain which statement is correct in respect thereof. | The borehole terminated within the continental crust | The borehole penetrated through the mantle into the outer core | The borehole reached the base of the mantle | The borehole exceeded half the radius of the Earth
